{"duration_api_ms":62213,"stop_reason":"tool_use","session_id":"5f31750d-d4ec-4a1a-b75b-efce9e2002ba","total_cost_usd":0.149738,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":18853,"cache_read_input_tokens":22225,"output_tokens":6357,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2415},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":18853,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2013,"cache_read_input_tokens":18296,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4486,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4486},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":6213,"outputTokens":18,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.006303,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000,"thinkingTokens":0,"canonicalModel":"claude-haiku-4-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"},"claude-sonnet-5":{"inputTokens":4,"outputTokens":6357,"cacheReadInputTokens":22225,"cacheCreationInputTokens":18853,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.143435,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":64000,"thinkingTokens":2415,"canonicalModel":"claude-sonnet-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":3,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"summary\":\"The attempt never reached PostgreSQL replication configuration. node2 and node3 had PostgreSQL 16 already installed and running with default (localhost-only, peer/trust) settings, but node1 hung indefinitely on privileged commands after a backgrounded `apt-get update`, a reboot was issued to recover it, and node1 never came back reachable before the cluster lease expired. The failure is at the infrastructure/reachability layer, not in any PostgreSQL or replication configuration step.\",\"evidence\":[{\"source\":\"command audit, node2/node3, 12:32:25Z\",\"finding\":\"`systemctl is-active postgresql; pg_lsclusters` returns 'active' and 'online' on both node2 and node3, confirming PostgreSQL 16 was already installed and running on the standby-designated nodes before node1 setup completed.\"},{\"source\":\"command audit, node2/node3, 12:32:34-35Z\",\"finding\":\"postgresql.conf shows `#listen_addresses = 'localhost'` (commented default, i.e. localhost-only) and pg_hba.conf shows only local/127.0.0.1/::1 entries with peer/scram auth — no replication-ready network configuration was in place yet on either standby.\"},{\"source\":\"command audit, node1, 12:23:36Z-12:30:37Z\",\"finding\":\"Four consecutive node1 commands (a backgrounded `apt-get update`, then three separate `sudo -n true` variants) each failed after ~125s with 'AntriebMCPError: ... 120-second Proxy Read Timeout', indicating node1 stopped responding to exec calls, specifically around privileged/sudo operations, for at least ~7 minutes.\"},{\"source\":\"command audit, node1, 12:32:13Z\",\"finding\":\"Executor issued `nohup reboot` on node1, which returned successfully (return_code 0, 'reboot_issued'), suggesting the exec channel itself was still functional at that moment even though sudo checks had been timing out.\"},{\"source\":\"command audit, node1, 12:32:16Z-12:46:21Z\",\"finding\":\"14 sequential poll attempts ('poll1' through 'poll14') on node1 over ~14 minutes all failed with 'Connection reset by peer' — node1 never became reachable again after the reboot was issued.\"},{\"source\":\"command audit, node2/node3, 12:33:14Z-12:46:18Z\",\"finding\":\"node2 and node3 were left idle in sleep loops (20s, 45s, 90s, 120s x4) while waiting for node1 to recover; no further PostgreSQL configuration commands were attempted on them during this window.\"},{\"source\":\"attempt termination metadata\",\"finding\":\"Cluster lease expired at 2026-09-08T12:47:00Z, about 39 seconds after the last failed reachability poll on node1 (poll14 at 12:46:21Z).\"}],\"likely_causes\":[{\"cause\":\"node1 became unresponsive to privileged (sudo) operations during/after a backgrounded apt-get update, and the subsequent reboot did not bring it back reachable within the remaining lease time — an infrastructure/VM-availability failure that consumed the entire attempt window before any replication configuration was attempted on any node.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"},{\"cause\":\"The apt-get update or a related package-management process on node1 hung (e.g. dpkg/apt lock contention with cloud-init or unattended-upgrades still running at boot), which is why later sudo checks also blocked — but no diagnostic (journalctl, ps, dpkg lock check) was run before the reboot was issued, so this is unconfirmed.\",\"confidence\":\"medium\"},{\"cause\":\"An underlying host or network-level fault caused node1's VM to fail to fully come back up after the reboot, independent of anything PostgreSQL- or apt-related — cannot be distinguished from the OS-level hang hypothesis with the evidence available.\",\"confidence\":\"low\"}],\"next_attempt_guidance\":[\"On a fresh cluster, verify each node's basic reachability with a cheap non-privileged probe (e.g. `echo alive`, `hostname`) before and interleaved with any sudo/apt operation, so an exec-layer hang can be distinguished from a sudo/PAM-specific hang early.\",\"Before running `apt-get update` in the background, check for lock contention (`fuser /var/lib/dpkg/lock-frontend`, `ps aux | grep -E 'apt|dpkg|unattended'`) so a stuck package manager can be identified and waited out or killed rather than discovered only via timeouts.\",\"If a node stops responding to privileged commands, don't stack multiple 120s-timeout sudo attempts before acting — cap the diagnostic budget (e.g. 2 attempts) then move to recovery (reboot) sooner.\",\"After issuing a reboot to recover an unresponsive node, set a bounded wait budget (e.g. 3-5 minutes) for it to become reachable again; if it doesn't, treat it as a platform-level failure and reprovision or escalate rather than polling until the lease expires.\",\"Since node2 and node3 already had PostgreSQL 16 installed with default localhost-only settings, on the next attempt prioritize getting node1 to the same baseline first (or provision/verify all three nodes in parallel) before spending time on any single node — the whole attempt was lost waiting on one node while the other two sat idle and ready.\",\"No evidence in this attempt bears on the actual replication setup (listen_addresses, pg_hba.conf replication rules, primary_conninfo/standby.signal, pg_basebackup, or failover/promotion logic) — treat that entire part of the task as untested, not previously attempted-and-failed.\"]}","structured_output":{"summary":"The attempt never reached PostgreSQL replication configuration. node2 and node3 had PostgreSQL 16 already installed and running with default (localhost-only, peer/trust) settings, but node1 hung indefinitely on privileged commands after a backgrounded `apt-get update`, a reboot was issued to recover it, and node1 never came back reachable before the cluster lease expired. The failure is at the infrastructure/reachability layer, not in any PostgreSQL or replication configuration step.","evidence":[{"source":"command audit, node2/node3, 12:32:25Z","finding":"`systemctl is-active postgresql; pg_lsclusters` returns 'active' and 'online' on both node2 and node3, confirming PostgreSQL 16 was already installed and running on the standby-designated nodes before node1 setup completed."},{"source":"command audit, node2/node3, 12:32:34-35Z","finding":"postgresql.conf shows `#listen_addresses = 'localhost'` (commented default, i.e. localhost-only) and pg_hba.conf shows only local/127.0.0.1/::1 entries with peer/scram auth — no replication-ready network configuration was in place yet on either standby."},{"source":"command audit, node1, 12:23:36Z-12:30:37Z","finding":"Four consecutive node1 commands (a backgrounded `apt-get update`, then three separate `sudo -n true` variants) each failed after ~125s with 'AntriebMCPError: ... 120-second Proxy Read Timeout', indicating node1 stopped responding to exec calls, specifically around privileged/sudo operations, for at least ~7 minutes."},{"source":"command audit, node1, 12:32:13Z","finding":"Executor issued `nohup reboot` on node1, which returned successfully (return_code 0, 'reboot_issued'), suggesting the exec channel itself was still functional at that moment even though sudo checks had been timing out."},{"source":"command audit, node1, 12:32:16Z-12:46:21Z","finding":"14 sequential poll attempts ('poll1' through 'poll14') on node1 over ~14 minutes all failed with 'Connection reset by peer' — node1 never became reachable again after the reboot was issued."},{"source":"command audit, node2/node3, 12:33:14Z-12:46:18Z","finding":"node2 and node3 were left idle in sleep loops (20s, 45s, 90s, 120s x4) while waiting for node1 to recover; no further PostgreSQL configuration commands were attempted on them during this window."},{"source":"attempt termination metadata","finding":"Cluster lease expired at 2026-09-08T12:47:00Z, about 39 seconds after the last failed reachability poll on node1 (poll14 at 12:46:21Z)."}],"likely_causes":[{"cause":"node1 became unresponsive to privileged (sudo) operations during/after a backgrounded apt-get update, and the subsequent reboot did not bring it back reachable within the remaining lease time — an infrastructure/VM-availability failure that consumed the entire attempt window before any replication configuration was attempted on any node.","confidence":"high"},{"cause":"The apt-get update or a related package-management process on node1 hung (e.g. dpkg/apt lock contention with cloud-init or unattended-upgrades still running at boot), which is why later sudo checks also blocked — but no diagnostic (journalctl, ps, dpkg lock check) was run before the reboot was issued, so this is unconfirmed.","confidence":"medium"},{"cause":"An underlying host or network-level fault caused node1's VM to fail to fully come back up after the reboot, independent of anything PostgreSQL- or apt-related — cannot be distinguished from the OS-level hang hypothesis with the evidence available.","confidence":"low"}],"next_attempt_guidance":["On a fresh cluster, verify each node's basic reachability with a cheap non-privileged probe (e.g. `echo alive`, `hostname`) before and interleaved with any sudo/apt operation, so an exec-layer hang can be distinguished from a sudo/PAM-specific hang early.","Before running `apt-get update` in the background, check for lock contention (`fuser /var/lib/dpkg/lock-frontend`, `ps aux | grep -E 'apt|dpkg|unattended'`) so a stuck package manager can be identified and waited out or killed rather than discovered only via timeouts.","If a node stops responding to privileged commands, don't stack multiple 120s-timeout sudo attempts before acting — cap the diagnostic budget (e.g. 2 attempts) then move to recovery (reboot) sooner.","After issuing a reboot to recover an unresponsive node, set a bounded wait budget (e.g. 3-5 minutes) for it to become reachable again; if it doesn't, treat it as a platform-level failure and reprovision or escalate rather than polling until the lease expires.","Since node2 and node3 already had PostgreSQL 16 installed with default localhost-only settings, on the next attempt prioritize getting node1 to the same baseline first (or provision/verify all three nodes in parallel) before spending time on any single node — the whole attempt was lost waiting on one node while the other two sat idle and ready.","No evidence in this attempt bears on the actual replication setup (listen_addresses, pg_hba.conf replication rules, primary_conninfo/standby.signal, pg_basebackup, or failover/promotion logic) — treat that entire part of the task as untested, not previously attempted-and-failed."]},"ttft_ms":28384,"type":"result","duration_ms":61099,"uuid":"4ccc1a65-000a-48e0-b77c-8a7d4ce96e1d","ttft_stream_ms":879,"time_to_request_ms":47,"first_content_frame_ms":1398,"queued_turn_count":0}
